#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tracker - attach, report one position, sleep, in as little charge as possible.

Project 15's router keeps a modem on a network and is judged by uptime.
This program is judged by charge per report, so almost every decision here
is the opposite of that one's. It does not keep a bearer. It does not poll.
It does not run a daemon that always knows the modem's state, because
knowing costs a wake-up and the modem is meant to be asleep.

The shape, which is the deliverable:

    OFF ---PWRKEY---> BOOTING ---AT ok---> CONFIGURED
                                                |
                                          attach|
                      +------------------ REGISTERED <--------+
                      |                         |             |
               report |                         | PSM granted |
                      v                         v             |
                   SENDING ---ack--->        ASLEEP ----------+
                      |                     (TAU timer)
                      | no ack, after the whole CoAP
                      | retransmission schedule
                      v
                   BACKOFF ---> (the timer starts the next run)

Every transition is driven by an AT response or by a timer, and both are
things a test can supply.

There are two seams, and they are at different layers.

The modem is reached only by writing a line and reading lines back, so a
fake modem is a port with a table of canned responses. `--port` takes any
readable and writable path, and the test hands it the slave side of a
pseudo-terminal: see tests/fake-modem.py and tests/tracker-state-test.sh.

A pty rather than the FIFO that seems simpler, for two reasons. A FIFO is
one-directional, and one process cannot open it read and write without
reading back its own traffic, so a bidirectional dialogue needs two of them
and a convention about which is which. And a pty slave is a character
device, so the termios setup below actually runs during the test, which a
FIFO would skip. The part most likely to be wrong is then the part the test
exercises rather than the part it steps around.

Everything else outside this process is invoked by name through PATH, which
is Project 15's `lte-watchdog` pattern and is here for the same reason: the
whole state machine then runs on a laptop with no modem and no GPIO. That
covers `tracker-gpio`, which owns PWRKEY and the report marker.

Why Python rather than the shell script Project 15 predicted. Two reasons,
both of them things the shell cannot do without adding a binary to the
image. CoAP is a binary protocol over UDP, and building its header is
sixteen lines of struct packing here against a helper program there. And
reading an AT response needs a read that times out, which POSIX shell has
no portable way to do. Nothing outside the standard library is imported, so
this costs python3-core in the image and no pyserial.

Why the CoAP packet is built here and handed to the modem as data, rather
than sent through a socket. There is no network interface. ModemManager is
absent by design, so there is no QMI and no PPP, and nothing has given the
kernel an `wwan0` to route through. The modem's own IP stack carries the
packet instead, reached over the same AT port. That is a consequence worth
stating plainly because it also shrinks the kernel question: this image
needs a USB serial driver and nothing else. No PPP, no CDC Ethernet.

SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
"""

